A||N||6||FieroJoe||Positive Trigger Power Door Locks?||||1||
Z||000000||FieroJoe||02-04-2001||12:09 AM||||While looking in my installation manual for my keyless entry/alarm system, it says I must know what style of power door locks exist in my car.  It shows diagrams for many different types of lock systems, with two that could possibly be for Fieros.  It lists GM under reverse polarity, and also says "Many GM" under Positive Trigger.  The diagram for the positive trigger shows a box for "factory power locking relays" which I know that Fieros have.  I know that the existing relay must reverse polarity in order to operate the locking soleniod properly.  So would that make Fiero pwr lock systems positive trigger, relying on the relay to switch polarity (to operate solenoids correctly) when they have voltage applied to the correct relay coil?<P>TIA!<P>------------------<BR>FieroJoe<BR>c311t@yahoo.com<BR>AIM: Fiero87311, Snowboarder0311<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroJoe||02-04-2001||09:47 PM||||back to top!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||02-04-2001||10:53 PM||||The Fiero's are "positive trigger" All I use to do when I installed alarm systems was run the positive trigger wire from the alarm box to the side of the switch. you should have 3 wires. One goes to the lock side, one goes to the unlock side, and the 3rd gets hooked to +12V. (Battery not accessory)<BR>Positive trigger means it need 12V to activate the lock/unlock relays.<BR>Hope this helps.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 02-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||MoneypitGT||02-04-2001||11:07 PM||||The fiero locks are positive trigger. Reverse polarity are also called 5 wire which is usually on the trucks, and do NOT have factory relays they also rest at ground. All you need to do is splice into the black wire (unlock) and the light blue wire (lock) with the appropriate wire from your alarm/keyless. Those wires are located on the relays which are above the pass. side kick, up under the dash. <BR>    Chris<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/fieroproject/moneypitgt.gif">   <BR>2 '87 GT Projects<p>[This message has been edited by MoneypitGT (edited 02-04-2001).]||||reg||2
Z||000004||FieroJoe||02-05-2001||07:49 AM||||Thanks guys!  That's all I needed to know!||||reg||1
Z||000005||ToddAsh||02-07-2001||06:03 PM|||| Just put a Crimestopper keyless system in this weekend.Both lock and unlock on the unit were only negative output.Had to use two relays to get locks to work.Other than that it was easy. All wires under right side dash. Black/orange stripe is constant 12V,used radio lead for ignition 12V,light blue and black for lock/unlock.Works great.Passive locking,even has a flashing light for the dash.Hated to drill a hole.First scarring of my Fiero,but its time to enjoy it after 15 years in storage. Todd||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroJoe||02-07-2001||08:29 PM||||Yeah I know what you mean about not wanting to mangle things up.  I am still going to use the flashing LED, but I think I have a neat way to put it in the dash, and even make it say "security" or something, to give an appearance of a stock device.  I'll post pics as soon as it gets done.||||reg||1
